  "query_preview": "..The Pacific Northwest trough that brought a dry cold\nfront through northern Utah yesterday is centered over North\nDakota this evening, with a very dry west to northwest flow over\nUtah and southwest Wyoming. Thus, seeing clear skies and generally\nlight winds over much of the area. Highs dropped to up to 5F below\nseasonal normals over northern Utah, while portions of southern\nUtah saw another day of very hot temperatures. \n\nThe less hot weather will persist for another day Tuesday, with\neven southern Utah seeing a small amount of relief from the heat.\nFlow aloft will tend to decrease but should remain elevated enough\nto bring breezy winds to the San Rafael Swell, combining with the\ndry airmass to bring another round of critical fire weather\nconditions. \n\nAs high pressure rebuilds along the Pacific coast for the middle\nof next week and beyond, Utah and southwest Wyoming will see a\nsteady warming trend, most noticeable over northern portions of\nthe forecast area. By this weekend, there will be a high chance of\nall northern Utah valley locations seeing highs of 100F or more\nand will need to monitor for an increased threat of heat related\nillness. Over southern Utah, there is a decent chance that the\nposition of the ridge will allow some monsoon moisture to return\nnorthward, which would limit warming and perhaps bring a few\nafternoon showers and thunderstorms to the higher terrain.\nGuidance is increasingly showing a more substantial return of\nmonsoon moisture early next week as the center of the ridge moves\nback to the Four Corners region.",
